WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:05.160
Veteran English actor Oliver Reed died before
finishing all of his scenes for Ridley Scott's

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.900
2000 epic Gladiator.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.820
This forced Scott to engage in some digital
trickery with outtakes to depict the death

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.580
of Reed's character, gladiator handler Proximo.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.240
But the circumstances surrounding Reed's death
serve to illustrate two things about the man:

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.110
he was every bit as tough as his onscreen
persona suggested, and he was a bit too fond

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.260
of liquor for his own good.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.660
The film was shot on location in Malta, and
on the night before he was to shoot his final

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.860
scenes, Reed celebrated by dropping in on
a local pub.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:39.780
He put down eight pints of lager before moving
on to a dozen double shots of rum and half

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:40.780
a bottle of whiskey.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:46.020
Then he proceeded to take on a crew of British
Navy sailors in an arm-wrestling contest,

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:49.040
defeating them all before finally preparing
to retire for the night.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.400
But after decades of such behavior, Reed's
body had finally had enough.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.880
He suffered a massive heart attack, and dropped
dead on the spot.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.760
The pub where he died has come to be known
as "Ollie's Last Pub."

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:05.360
Fans the world over come through to browse
its collection of memorabilia, have a pint

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.300
or three, and pay tribute to one of the most
legendary tough guys in film history.

00:01:10.318 --> 00:01:12.718
But his complete list of credits is longer
than your arm.

00:01:12.918 --> 00:01:18.918
Among them were the 1973 version of The Three
Musketeers and its 1989 sequel, The Return

00:01:18.918 --> 00:01:23.218
of the Musketeers, both directed by Kinnear's
frequent collaborator Richard Lester.

00:01:23.518 --> 00:01:26.218
Alas, the latter ending up being his final
role.

00:01:26.698 --> 00:01:31.018
Kinnear was injured in a fall from a horse
during the shoot in Spain, and he was subsequently

00:01:31.018 --> 00:01:32.718
admitted to a hospital in Madrid.

00:01:33.198 --> 00:01:37.058
His injuries weren't considered life-threatening,
but they were apparently too much for his

00:01:37.058 --> 00:01:37.558
body.

00:01:37.798 --> 00:01:42.118
He suffered a heart attack the next day while
still in the hospital, and doctors weren't

00:01:42.118 --> 00:01:42.938
able to save him.

00:01:43.158 --> 00:01:48.118
Kinnear's untimely death had a profound effect
on the film's cast and crew, but none were

00:01:48.118 --> 00:01:51.018
so affected as Lester, who had lost a dear
friend.

00:01:51.638 --> 00:01:55.858
1987's Million Dollar Mystery was as much
a gimmick as it was a film.

00:01:56.098 --> 00:02:00.698
Its cast of zany characters spent the entire
runtime hunting down a series of three cash

00:02:00.698 --> 00:02:04.618
stashes of one million dollars hidden somewhere
in the United States.

00:02:04.758 --> 00:02:09.048
The ending revealed that the third stash of
hidden loot was totally real, as it challenged

00:02:09.048 --> 00:02:12.278
audiences to use clues from the film to determine
its location.

00:02:12.568 --> 00:02:17.858
The movie was widely ignored when it wasn't
being roundly panned, but its most unfortunate

00:02:17.858 --> 00:02:21.878
legacy lies in the accident that took the
life of stuntman Dar Robinson.

00:02:22.178 --> 00:02:26.198
Widely considered by his peers to be one of
the gutsiest stunt workers alive, Robinson

00:02:26.198 --> 00:02:31.218
held a number of insane records, including
the highest fall ever put on film.

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:36.238
He had somehow never suffered an on-set injury
in his career, but that changed in the most

00:02:36.238 --> 00:02:40.598
tragic fashion possible during the shooting
of a routine motorcycle chase sequence.

00:02:41.028 --> 00:02:45.698
Robinson went into a curve at a high speed
on his Honda 600 dirt bike, losing control

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.058
and skidding off the road.

00:02:47.378 --> 00:02:52.538
He was separated from the bike, thrown down
an embankment and impaled by a sagebrush branch.

00:02:52.918 --> 00:02:55.798
He was airlifted to a hospital, but his injuries
ended up killing him.

00:02:56.718 --> 00:03:01.238
John Jordan was among the most fearless camera
operators who ever lived, having developed

00:03:01.238 --> 00:03:05.048
his own perilous technique of getting the
kind of aerial shots that simply shouldn't

00:03:05.048 --> 00:03:06.058
have been possible.

00:03:06.458 --> 00:03:10.838
Secured only by a harness, with his feet resting
on the skid of a helicopter, he would lean

00:03:10.838 --> 00:03:13.638
out into the blue to secure his amazing shots.

00:03:13.778 --> 00:03:18.178
It was during the filming of one such aerial
sequence for the James Bond film You Only

00:03:18.178 --> 00:03:23.898
Live Twice that he came a little too close
to one of his subjects, a Bell 47 helicopter.

00:03:24.318 --> 00:03:29.218
One of its blades struck and almost removed
his foot, which eventually had to be amputated.

00:03:29.558 --> 00:03:33.778
But Jordan was undeterred, and he went right
back to work doing what he did best.

00:03:33.958 --> 00:03:39.098
This gung-ho attitude finally caught up with
him in 1969 while shooting Catch-22.

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:44.178
Jordan was acting as second-unit director,
shooting from the open door of a B25 bomber.

00:03:44.278 --> 00:03:49.418
Once again, a passing aircraft got a little
bit too close, but rather than making contact,

00:03:49.418 --> 00:03:53.618
it created a freak gust of wind that threw
the B25 off to one side.

00:03:54.078 --> 00:03:58.438
Jordan lost his balance, got too close to
the door, and was promptly sucked out.

00:03:58.718 --> 00:04:01.878
He plummeted to his death 4,000 feet into
the ocean.

00:04:02.598 --> 00:04:07.158
While not a household name, director, actor,
and stunt driver Toby Halicki won himself

00:04:07.158 --> 00:04:11.558
a cult following in the 1970s by virtue of
being preternaturally gifted at the art of

00:04:11.558 --> 00:04:13.138
filming car chases and crashes.

00:04:13.558 --> 00:04:18.678
His sophomore effort, 1974's Gone in 60 Seconds,
is a prime example of this gift.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:24.338
But during the filming of its 1989 sequel,
Halicki met his end in a bizarre accident

00:04:24.338 --> 00:04:27.798
that strangely had nothing to do with his
insanely dangerous stunt work.

00:04:28.458 --> 00:04:32.418
While filming in Tonawanda, New York, the
crew was setting up to shoot the collapse

00:04:32.418 --> 00:04:37.318
of a water tower for a scene that Tonawanda
officials had tried mightily to prevent.

00:04:37.598 --> 00:04:42.558
Halicki had been forced to take out an $8
million insurance policy in order to get them

00:04:42.558 --> 00:04:47.418
to relent, and he had announced his plans
to sue the city upon the film's completion.

00:04:47.878 --> 00:04:51.258
But as it turned out, he probably should've
paid more attention to their concerns.

00:04:51.258 --> 00:04:55.838
The tower collapsed prematurely, causing an
attached steel cable to snap.

00:04:56.098 --> 00:05:01.198
The cable then knocked over a nearby telephone
pole, which fell directly on top of Halicki.

00:05:01.638 --> 00:05:06.248
He was treated by EMTs at the scene to no
avail, and he was pronounced dead on arrival

00:05:06.248 --> 00:05:07.338
at the local hospital.

00:05:07.718 --> 00:05:12.658
Halicki's widow Denice served as an executive
producer on the 2000 remake of Gone in 60

00:05:12.658 --> 00:05:15.658
Seconds as a means of preserving her husband's
legacy.

00:05:16.398 --> 00:05:21.138
2008's The Dark Knight featured some incredible
stunt work in conjunction with spectacular,

00:05:21.138 --> 00:05:23.578
and sometimes dangerous, practical effects.

00:05:23.878 --> 00:05:28.598
Among the stunt performers working on the
film was Conway Wickliffe, a 41-year-old veteran

00:05:28.598 --> 00:05:29.238
of the industry.

00:05:29.608 --> 00:05:34.718
While shooting a portion of the Tumbler/Batcycle
chase sequence, Wickliffe was in the process

00:05:34.718 --> 00:05:37.818
of capturing a fairly standard shot when tragedy
struck.

00:05:38.198 --> 00:05:41.298
He was filming a stunt car while hanging out
the window of an SUV.

00:05:41.498 --> 00:05:46.958
His vehicle's driver inexplicably veered off
course, failing to follow his prescribed route

00:05:46.958 --> 00:05:51.918
and instead plowing through a grassy area,
a detour that ended with a crash into a tree.

00:05:51.978 --> 00:05:56.938
Despite the fact that the SUV was only going
about 20 miles per hour, Wickliffe sustained

00:05:56.938 --> 00:05:59.878
injuries serious enough that he was pronounced
dead at the scene.

00:06:00.338 --> 00:06:05.938
His untimely demise was, unfortunately, a
harbinger of things to come, as star Heath

00:06:05.938 --> 00:06:10.338
Ledger died of an accidental drug overdose
shortly before the film's release.

00:06:10.518 --> 00:06:14.858
The shocking deaths of both men were memorialized
in The Dark Knight's closing credits.

00:06:15.518 --> 00:06:20.158
In 1978, residents of Lexington, Kentucky
were thrilled to have a real Hollywood production

00:06:20.158 --> 00:06:20.658
in town.

00:06:21.058 --> 00:06:25.178
It was Steel, starring and produced by Lexington
native Lee Majors.

00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:29.698
The film's climax called for its villain to
plummet from a skyscraper to his death, and

00:06:29.698 --> 00:06:33.438
the city's Kincaid Towers, which were nearing
completion, were just the ticket.

00:06:33.918 --> 00:06:36.788
The fall was performed by 27-year old A.J.

00:06:36.788 --> 00:06:41.577
Bakunas, who at one time held the record for
the highest fall, and the scene had been completed

00:06:41.578 --> 00:06:44.558
with Bakunas jumping from the ninth story
into an airbag.

00:06:45.008 --> 00:06:49.438
But then Bakunas got word that Dar Robinson
had recently broken his record, causing him

00:06:49.448 --> 00:06:55.128
to insist on shooting the scene again, making
the jump from the top of the 323-foot tower

00:06:55.128 --> 00:06:55.678
this time.

00:06:55.878 --> 00:06:58.958
The on-set physician tried desperately to
halt the attempt.

00:06:59.298 --> 00:07:02.898
But Bakunas, intent on regaining his record,
wouldn't change his mind.

00:07:03.718 --> 00:07:08.168
He performed the fall, and struck the airbag
squarely in the prescribed area, only to have

00:07:08.168 --> 00:07:08.938
it rupture.

00:07:09.358 --> 00:07:14.158
Amazingly, despite having just fallen hundreds
of feet essentially onto concrete, Bakunas

00:07:14.158 --> 00:07:15.478
initially clung to life.

00:07:16.118 --> 00:07:20.068
But although his head and internal organs
had escaped serious injury, his lungs had

00:07:20.068 --> 00:07:21.398
taken too much damage.

00:07:21.678 --> 00:07:22.898
He died the next day.

00:07:23.638 --> 00:07:29.558
In February 2014, a 20-person film crew showed
up for work on the biopic Midnight Rider,

00:07:29.558 --> 00:07:31.698
the life story of rocker Gregg Allman.

00:07:32.178 --> 00:07:36.938
Director Randall Miller had them shoot scenes
on an active train track, assuring them that

00:07:36.938 --> 00:07:40.418
it was in use only by two trains, both of
which had already gone by.

00:07:40.738 --> 00:07:45.298
In the unlikely event that another train appeared,
they were told they'd have only sixty seconds

00:07:45.298 --> 00:07:46.258
to clear the track.

00:07:46.778 --> 00:07:51.588
That ended up being not nearly enough time,
as a third train made an unexpected appearance

00:07:51.588 --> 00:07:52.758
in the middle of the shoot.

00:07:53.258 --> 00:07:57.218
The locomotive was traveling at a speed close
to 60 miles per hour.

00:07:57.818 --> 00:08:01.878
It struck a bed which had been affixed to
the tracks, sending debris flying.

00:08:01.958 --> 00:08:07.038
27-year old camera operator Sarah Jones was
somehow pushed into the path of the train,

00:08:07.038 --> 00:08:08.658
which struck and killed her instantly.

00:08:09.338 --> 00:08:13.608
Miller subsequently became the first filmmaker
to be convicted on a criminal charge related

00:08:13.608 --> 00:08:14.838
to events on a film set.

00:08:15.628 --> 00:08:20.258
He pled guilty to involuntary manslaughter
and was sentenced to two years in prison,

00:08:20.258 --> 00:08:23.538
with eight years of probation to follow, while
Midnight Rider was canceled.

